WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.450
DEBORA ELKIN: Hello, and
welcome to this session.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.410
My name is Debora Elkin, and
I'm a technical solutions

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.420
consultant with Google Cloud.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.030
I'll be presenting this
session with Warren Vella.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.280
He's an enterprise
integration architect

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.870
with the Australian
Energy Market Operator.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:24.500
In this session, we
will see how APIs

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.980
can help you unlock the
power of your existing data

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.710
and how the Australian
Energy Market Operator is

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:33.260
leveraging Apache to do so.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:36.125
We will also see
AEMO's APIs in action.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.580
We all know that this is the
age of connective experiences.

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.010
Your customers, or your
customers' customers,

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.650
want to interact with
applications and data

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:49.890
in different ways, in
different channels,

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:51.640
on different platforms.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.220
These interactions are
constantly changing,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.190
and everything's connected
with everything else.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:01.300
APIs are key enablers
of digital experiences.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.110
Think how pervasive Google
Maps or how booking systems

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:07.670
have made our lives easier.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:11.800
Of course, this data involved
in all digital experiences,

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.010
whether it be available flights
on a travel booking system

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:18.040
or your bank account
details in online banking

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.850
or just about anything
you can think of, really.

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:25.930
However, the data you need to
build these digital experiences

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.410
is often buried deep within a
company's systems of record.

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:33.880
It could be in data
warehouse, data lakes, files

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.030
that could be scattered
everywhere, disparate systems,

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.210
legacy systems
have to connect to.

00:01:40.210 --> 00:01:44.110
This data is most likely
locked away, hard to get at,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:48.300
and it takes a lot of
time to surface it.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.340
So somewhere, there is a system
of record, holding data that

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:53.840
is hard to get at.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.440
It can take months or years
to make the changes required

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.070
to make that data available.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.540
But customer or consumer's
expectations are high.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.490
They want the digital
experience that

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.750
is underpinned by that data,
and they want it quick.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.720
This is where API
management fits in.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.210
It fills the gap between
the systems of record

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:19.620
and the requirements of
new digital experiences.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.850
It helps organizations build a
bridge between legacy systems

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.440
and mother applications.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.710
And it can build that bridge
in a secure, reliable,

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.440
and scalable way.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.860
So let's talk about Apigee.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.120
Apigee is Google Cloud's API
management platform, not just

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.090
an API gateway, and
gives you all the tools

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:46.140
you need for unlocking
your data via APIs.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.280
Apigee enables digital
business acceleration

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:53.070
with a unified and complete
platform, purpose-built

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.240
for the digital economy.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:59.010
Apigee makes it easier to
manage the entire digital volume

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:02.250
chain with API services,
developer services,

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.630
and analytic services.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:08.070
Let's start at the
bottom with API services.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.060
This is the API runtime
and mediation engine.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.990
API services lets you manage
the connection between the API

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.960
consumers and the
back-end service.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.920
You have complete control
over the request and response.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.910
You can add security, transform
requests and responses,

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.860
orchestrate calls
across multiple systems

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:33.060
impose rate limits and
quotas and prevent people

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.270
from using your services.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:40.080
API analytics helps you
measure, tune, and monitor

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.120
your API program.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.560
It enables end-to-end visibility
across the digital value chain

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.290
with developer analytics,
operational and business

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.200
metrics, reports
about app performance,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.780
and custom reports
that you may need.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.540
All are necessary to
monitor, measure, and manage

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.450
success of your API program.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:04.200
Developer services enable a
great developer and community

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.790
experience that accelerates API
adoption, simplifies learning,

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:12.330
and increases the
business value of APIs.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.980
It provides a developer
portal for your API consumers,

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.750
along with features for
packaging, monetizing

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.959
your services and data.

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:23.580
When you're sharing
your data, you

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.540
want to make sure you
do this in a secure way.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.330
When we talk about
security, there

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.590
are many different aspects.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:35.930
For instance, data should
only reach the right audience.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:40.160
This is where authentication
and authorization come in.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.210
In that respect, Apigee
supports widely used standards,

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:47.850
such as OAuth and
OpenID Connect.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.760
You also don't want
anyone snooping

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.320
around when the
data is in transit.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.730
This is where encryption
plays a key role.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.310
Apigee supports TLS,
mutual TLS, and can

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.000
support different configuration
of these protocols

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.210
when communicating with API
clients and back-end services

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.710
that hold it back.

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:11.000
For example, you may configure
one-way TLS on the client side,

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.700
but mutual TLS list for
one of your back-ends

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.570
and one-way TLS for another.

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.050
Auditing is also very important.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:22.070
You need to know who has
access to what data, who

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:26.250
has made a change to the
API that exposes that data.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:30.050
Apigee auditing and logging
capabilities have you covered.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.080
Also, is this sensitive data?

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:32.780
No problem.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:36.410
Use an Apigee extension
to quickly redact data

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.830
with GCP data loss
prevention services.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:44.010
You could potentially think of
making money out of your data

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:44.850
as well--

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.710
that is, if you wish to.

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:50.790
Of course, not all APIs should
necessarily be monetized.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.550
There are APIs that
built consumer experience

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.800
or drive indirect value.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.580
They should be
free-- for instance,

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.330
think about API
for ticket booking,

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:04.950
finding store locations,
product availability, et cetera.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:08.100
On the other hand, if
your APIs help developers

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.800
build apps that
generate revenue,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.920
you may consider
charging for those APIs--

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.350
for example, APIs
that provide weather

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.290
data or messaging services.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.800
Apigee API monetization
offers multiple options

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.870
to set up charge plans.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:29.010
You can configure fee-based
plans, volume-based plans.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.920
You can set up revenue
sharing with developers,

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:34.260
freemium models, et cetera.

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:37.500
It also allows you to check
monetization plan limits

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.670
during API execution.

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:45.110
In short, whether you want to
build new digital experiences,

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:49.280
create a partner ecosystem, or
apply artificial intelligence

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:52.730
or machine learning to your
data, unlocking your data

00:06:52.730 --> 00:06:53.870
is the key.

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:57.080
And APIs are the
way to achieve this.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:59.870
We will now see how the
Australian Energy Market

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:03.190
Operator is unlocking
its data and putting it

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:06.720
to effective work
in an ecosystem.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.582
I'll now hand over to Warren.

00:07:10.582 --> 00:07:11.790
WARREN VELLA: Thank you, Deb.

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.250
Hello, my name is
Warren Vella, and I

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.530
am the enterprise
integration architect

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.060
at the Australian
Energy Market Operator.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:22.230
Today I'd like to talk
through how AEMO has partnered

00:07:22.230 --> 00:07:24.810
with Google and
the Apigee platform

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:29.040
to unlock the power of our
existing data through APIs.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.900
We'll take a look
at who AEMO is,

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:34.470
some of the unique challenges
the energy industry is facing,

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.020
and how APIs have
been instrumental

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.320
in our digital transformation.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:43.780
So where does AEMO sit within
the Australian energy market?

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:46.920
AEMO is responsible for
the day-to-day operations

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:49.020
of the Australian
energy market, which

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:51.900
is made up of multiple
markets covering both gas

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.550
and electricity.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:56.130
On one side, AEMO manages
the physical named

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.460
grid, the transmission
network, the large electricity

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.730
towers that you may have
seen around the country.

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:06.750
On the other side, AEMO operates
the financial energy markets

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:10.560
to enable energy flow all
the way from generation

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.990
to the customer
households via retailers.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:18.070
As you can see, some of the
actors in the energy markets

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.300
are typical,
large-scale generators,

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.180
such as coal power
plants and wind

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:25.250
farms, all the way to
metadata providers, who

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.040
are responsible for sending
the metadata from households

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.450
to AEMO.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:31.820
Sometimes this is
automated via smart meters,

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:34.130
and sometimes this
is manually read.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:36.890
One of the biggest shifts we
are seeing in the energy market

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.650
is the active role of
the customer, who is now

00:08:39.650 --> 00:08:42.570
also playing a critical
part of the grid,

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:46.710
especially as power is starting
to flow two-way, as into houses

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.470
and now out of houses.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.720
Let's take a look at some
of the technology challenges

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.415
that the energy industry faces.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:57.790
If we look deeper into the
national electricity market,

00:08:57.790 --> 00:09:00.100
which was built
over 20 years ago,

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:03.100
the systems are largely are
connected over either SCADA

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:06.740
operational systems over private
networks or market data--

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:10.210
for example, dispatch data,
which is an instruction sent

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:12.150
to a generator
every five minutes--

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.910
or bids and offers
into the market,

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:16.340
which is received from
generators and retailers,

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.380
is still sent over to AEMO
by batch-based systems

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.550
over private networks.

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:24.590
With the increase of DER
penetration across Australia,

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:27.490
which is leading the world,
we have a unique opportunity

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:33.460
to unlock the value in our
data over modern protocols.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.033
And so what can
AEMO do about this?

00:09:36.033 --> 00:09:37.450
We have a unique
opportunity where

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:40.690
we are seeing an intersection
between the energy industry

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:44.000
transformation and our
digital transformation.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.240
And as part of our
digital transformation,

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.070
we are tapping into
these opportunities

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:51.700
to enable new channels to really
lower the barrier to entry,

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.650
promoting further
competition in the market.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.740
And as we digitize
our systems, we

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.750
are looking to foster innovation
within the energy market,

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.710
especially in the space
of distributed energy.

00:10:02.710 --> 00:10:05.080
More importantly, we
are seeing the rise

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:08.110
of prosumers, who are looking
to take control of their energy

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:12.470
profile and play an active part
in this energy transformation.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:15.160
And so working with
Apigee has enabled

00:10:15.160 --> 00:10:20.320
us to start this journey as we
look to reach a wider audience.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:23.470
Our new Apigee development
portal arms us with the tools

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.950
to create a world-class
developer experience,

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:30.490
accelerate API adoption,
simplify learning,

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:33.910
and increase the business
value of our APIs.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:36.700
Developers are the key
consumers of our APIs,

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:39.280
and we want to provide them
with a great experience

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.860
and make it really easy
for them to discover,

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:45.850
understand, test, and
get going with our APIs,

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:48.040
from having an interactive
sandbox to test

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.890
our APIs to letting developers
manage their own keys

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:53.550
and access to the APIs,
and not necessarily

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:56.800
in an open-for-all approach
but with the right guardrails

00:10:56.800 --> 00:10:58.180
that Apigee offers.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:00.790
Our Apigee developer
portal has all the tools

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:03.880
a developer needs to
integrate with us.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.400
Let's take a look at some
of our large-scale programs

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:11.392
and opportunities to unlock
the value of our data.

00:11:11.392 --> 00:11:12.850
The energy industry
is moving along

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:15.130
with many programs, centered
around a common theme

00:11:15.130 --> 00:11:17.500
in distributed energy resources.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.395
Starting with the distributed
energy resource register,

00:11:20.395 --> 00:11:24.430
DER register allows
registration of DER assets

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:26.050
sitting behind the meter--

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:29.460
for example, rooftop solar
panels and household batteries.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:32.260
APIs have enabled for
this data to be registered

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:35.110
via the existing tools
and mobile applications

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:38.530
that electricians and
installers already used to.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:40.650
The virtual power
plant trial has

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:43.230
started to advance
the integration of DER

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.240
into the national
electricity market,

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.100
allowing consumers
to group together

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:49.500
to become a virtual power plant.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.420
APIs have enabled for the
management of these VPPs,

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:55.700
especially in the transfer
of telemetry data.

00:11:55.700 --> 00:11:57.880
And in the space of
five-minute settlements,

00:11:57.880 --> 00:12:00.490
the market is moving to more
granular data and settlement

00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:01.960
down to five minutes.

00:12:01.960 --> 00:12:05.320
APIs have enabled for meter
data at five-minute resolution

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:08.770
to be sent across the country
to aim as metadata management

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:10.600
database.

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.250
Next we'll take a
look at the CDR.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:15.490
The CDR is a national
legislative program,

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.860
which is running across
multiple industries,

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:20.590
with the energy
industry next in line.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:24.280
The CDR aims to give consumers
better access to their own data

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:28.150
and also allows competition by
using a data-driven, innovation

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:29.240
approach.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.380
So let's take a look at
how we are using the Apigee

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.800
platform to support
the CDR as a use case.

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:39.010
Some of the key areas of
why Apigee has been selected

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:41.660
was the choice of
implementation options,

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:43.810
especially with the
hybrid option giving us

00:12:43.810 --> 00:12:47.167
even more flexibility on
where to host our APIs,

00:12:47.167 --> 00:12:49.000
whether that is in the
Google Cloud platform

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.100
or in our private cloud.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:53.350
Data sovereignty is
especially important

00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:56.320
as we operate national
critical infrastructure,

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:57.970
and the flexibility
of the platform

00:12:57.970 --> 00:12:59.886
allows us to host
the platform to many

00:12:59.886 --> 00:13:04.040
of our strict obligations for
the protection of our data.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.430
And so the main focus
of the Apigee platform

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:09.760
is to manage our
externally facing APIs.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.950
In the case of the
CDP, this allows

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:15.280
us to expose data from our
metadata management systems.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.300
And like most
organizations, we have

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:21.370
data spread over multiple cloud
platforms and on-prem systems,

00:13:21.370 --> 00:13:24.130
and Apigee allows us
to unify the experience

00:13:24.130 --> 00:13:26.710
when integrating with our APIs.

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:29.860
Apigee is truly enabling
our digital transformation

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:33.010
as we renovate our internal
systems from legacy integration

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:37.790
protocols to modern
API ecosystems.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:39.440
Now we'll take a
look at a live demo

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:41.600
and explore how the
Victorian government is

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:44.750
using our APIs to provide
a comparison service

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.210
for Australian consumers.

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:51.400
Here we have an example
of an external consumer.

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:54.130
The Victorian government
energy comparison website,

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:57.610
who was consuming
the AEMO's CDP APIs

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:01.300
to provide real-time metadata
retrieval from AEMO's metadata

00:14:01.300 --> 00:14:02.790
management systems.

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:05.590
And before the CDP
APIs were available,

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:08.540
the end user needed to
complete over 10 questions,

00:14:08.540 --> 00:14:12.100
and in some cases, a PDF
bill had to be uploaded.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:14.740
Now that the API's
available, the user only

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.510
needs to know their
national metering

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:18.820
identifier, or their
NMI, and the data

00:14:18.820 --> 00:14:21.680
is retrieved live from our API.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:24.820
I'll now hand it off to Deb to
walk us through a live demo.

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.830
DEBORA ELKIN: The demo
we're about to see

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:30.730
is from the point of view of the
consumer wanting to understand

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:33.160
which energy retailer
offers the best

00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:36.070
rates for the particular
electricity consumption

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:36.570
patterns.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.690
I start looking around,
and I find a public website

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:45.310
that has been set up by
the government of Victoria,

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:46.970
Australia.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:49.790
When this website
was first launched,

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:53.090
the website would estimate
the energy consumption pattern

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:56.480
based on a series of answers
provided by the consumer.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:14:59.270
This was the simplest
option but still

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:02.720
required a lot of
information to be provided.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:04.400
The other option
was for the consumer

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:08.520
to upload a PDF
copy of their bill.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.730
I will now start answering all
the questions I am being asked.

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.390
After providing
this information,

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:30.210
I also need to provide the
details of my last electricity

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:30.710
bill.

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:37.650
At this point, I can
finally see which

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:41.530
rates are the most convenient
to my particular situation.

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:45.490
But I had to answer more than
ten questions to get here.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:49.710
And still, it is
just an estimate.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:53.160
Let us now see how
APIs that expose data

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:56.280
can improve my user
experience, as well as provide

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:57.900
more accurate results.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.100
So let's start again.

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:10.890
This time I will choose
a different method

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:14.650
for building my energy
profile, quick and accurate.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:16.540
This looks promising.

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:19.120
All I need to provide is a NMI.

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.920
This is a unique identifier
for the smart meter installed

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:24.540
in my house.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:27.930
I can find it in my
electricity bill.

00:16:27.930 --> 00:16:31.740
This option is now
powered by AEMO.

00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:36.160
What this means is that
when I click on Get My Data,

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:40.410
this website uses an AEMO API
to retrieve the actual energy

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.770
consumption for my house
in the last 12 months.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:46.470
This provides a very
accurate picture

00:16:46.470 --> 00:16:48.015
on how I consume energy.

00:16:51.400 --> 00:16:55.300
Voila, I can now see the rates
best suited to my household

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.940
without having to go through
a lengthy questionnaire.

00:16:57.940 --> 00:16:59.980
I, as a consumer,
didn't even need

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:04.060
to see the raw data
coming back from the API.

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:06.670
This useful experience
is facilitated

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:09.700
by the API published by AEMO.

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:12.339
Looking at AEMO's API
portal, a developer

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:15.200
can easily find
the relevant APIs

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:19.470
and understand how to use them.

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:22.500
In this demo, we can
appreciate the advantages

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.690
of unlocking data via APIs.

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:28.380
This is a public
government website

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:31.830
that leverages information
held by AEMO to provide

00:17:31.830 --> 00:17:34.390
a much better user experience.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:37.950
Not only are the suggestions
more accurate to the consumer,

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:41.850
but also the consumer can see
them in a couple of clicks.

00:17:41.850 --> 00:17:44.230
Thank you for watching it.

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.430
WARREN VELLA: So what's
next for AEMO and Apigee?

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:51.210
One of our main focus areas is
to enable DER resources to be

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.240
integrated into the grid,
which will increase API

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.180
calls to millions per day
as we start to receive

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.095
more and more telemetry data.

00:17:59.095 --> 00:18:03.240
And for the CDP API, we are
looking to expand this service

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:06.300
and enable data service
holders to also provide

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:09.870
data via the CVR gateway model.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:12.740
We are always looking to reduce
our participant onboarding time

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:14.700
and pivoting off
our developer portal

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:17.130
to provide the right
amount of information

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:20.460
and further reduce the time
to first, "Hello world."

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:23.580
And of course, our
API product portfolio

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:27.420
is expanding as we continue to
transform our systems as APIs

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:30.420
become our primary
form of integration

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:33.400
for our critical market systems.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:34.950
Thank you for your
time, and I'll now

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.967
hand it back to Deb for
some closing remarks.

00:18:37.967 --> 00:18:39.300
DEBORA ELKIN: Thank you, Warren.

00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:43.770
Today we looked at how APIs
can be used to securely expose

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:46.470
your data and how the
Australian Energy Market

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:48.670
Operator is doing this.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.340
We want to thank you for
watching this session,

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:53.580
and if you'd like to
continue the conversation,

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:56.580
we have set up some
expert one-on-one sessions

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:58.640
over the next couple of weeks.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:01.140
And of course, we will
also be answering questions

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:02.880
you submit in the
question and answer

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:05.400
section for this session.

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.080
Thank you.

